Bootstrap: docker
From: python:3.12-slim

# Chai-1 0.6.1 -- structure prediction, on a torch that has sm_120.
#
# REBUILD of chai1_2 for Blackwell, 2026-11-03. chai1_2.def and chai1_1.def are
# kept beside this one because they are what every Chai-1 number this lab holds
# was produced with, and a recipe you can no longer read is provenance you no
# longer have.
#
# ── WHY, and what this deliberately breaks ────────────────────────────────
#
# chai_lab 0.6.1 declares `torch>=2.3.1,<2.7`. Inside that ceiling pip resolves
# torch 2.6.x with cu124 wheels, and chai1_2's own test.sh records the
# consequence as a measurement rather than an inference:
#   torch._C._cuda_getArchFlags() -> sm_50 sm_60 sm_70 sm_75 sm_80 sm_86 sm_90
# There is NO `compute_*` entry in that list, which is the part that decides
# this. A build that ships PTX for a lower arch can be JIT-compiled forward onto
# a newer device by the driver; this one ships none, so sm_120 is not a slow
# path here, it is no path. ColabFold 1.5.5 fails the same way for the same
# reason and the error is worth knowing because it is NOT "no kernel image":
# jax enumerates the device, then dies with CUDA_ERROR_INVALID_IMAGE on the
# first launch.
#
# 0.6.1 IS THE LAST RELEASE. Checked 2026-11-03: PyPI's latest is 0.6.1 and
# chaidiscovery/chai-lab's newest tag is v0.6.1, so there is no newer chai_lab
# whose pin admits a cu128 wheel. Waiting is not a strategy with a date on it.
#
# So this image installs chai_lab 0.6.1 and then REPLACES torch with
# 2.7.0+cu128, which is one minor version past the declared ceiling and the
# oldest build PyTorch publishes with sm_120 kernels. pip will say
#   chai-lab 0.6.1 requires torch<2.7,>=2.3.1, but you have torch 2.7.0+cu128
# and that warning is accurate and expected. This entry is a declared
# exception to an upstream pin, which is a thing to do knowingly and to gate on
# evidence, not a thing to do quietly.
#
# ── THE GATE. Do not treat a passing %test as permission to believe a fold. ──
#
# %test below proves the stack imports, the arch flags carry sm_120, the
# downloads dir is writable and kalign really aligns. It does NOT prove the
# chemistry survived a torch minor bump. Chai-1 is the one predictor in this
# lab's history whose output was wrong in a way confidence scores did not
# detect: ipTM moved 0.710 -> 0.698 between a hapten bonded to the warhead and
# the same hapten bonded to an aromatic ring carbon 12 A away. The consuming
# project therefore holds a regression over nine real folds of its own target
# asserting the peptide cysteine bonds 1.636-1.770 A from the warhead sulfur
# against 12.5-15.3 A for the decoy atom.
#
# A covalent fold from THIS image must land in that band before any result from
# it is admitted to a scientific decision. If it does not, the right conclusion
# is that torch 2.7 changed something chai_lab 0.6.1 relies on, and the entry
# goes back to chai1_2 on an L40S.
#
# Build:  chpc-tool build chai1

%labels
    Tool         chai1
    Version      0.6.1
    Upstream     https://github.com/chaidiscovery/chai-lab
    UpstreamRef  chai_lab==0.6.1 (PyPI), torch 2.7.0+cu128 forced over its pin
    Licence      Apache-2.0, code and weights
    Base         python:3.12-slim
    GPUArch      includes sm_120 -- asserted at build time, see %post
    Kalign       3.4.0 (Debian trixie) -- REQUIRED for templates
    Rebuild      2026-11-03, torch 2.7.0+cu128 for sm_120
    Maintainer   Craik Lab, UCSF

%post
    set -eux
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends git ca-certificates kalign
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

    # chai_lab first, so its own resolution happens normally and we can see
    # exactly what we are overriding.
    pip install --no-cache-dir chai_lab==0.6.1
    python -c "import torch; print('resolved by chai_lab:', torch.__version__)"

    # Then the override. The cu128 index carries torch and its nvidia-* runtime
    # wheels, so nothing else has to resolve from there.
    pip install --no-cache-dir --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu128 \
        "torch==2.7.0+cu128"

    # torch's install can drag numpy forward; chai_lab wants 1.x.
    pip install --no-cache-dir "numpy<2"

    # ── pyarrow, and it is a BUG FIX rather than a convenience ─────────────
    #
    # chai_lab reads and writes MSAs as parquet (*.aligned.pqt) and does NOT
    # declare a parquet engine, so a pip install of chai_lab alone produces an
    # image that folds single-sequence and dies the moment an MSA is supplied:
    #   ImportError: Unable to find a usable engine; tried using: 'pyarrow',
    #   'fastparquet'
    # raised out of pandas.DataFrame.to_parquet.
    #
    # MEASURED 2026-11-03, and the mechanism is worse than a missing package.
    # pyarrow IS installed -- chai_lab's own dependency tree pulls
    # pyarrow 26.0.1 -- and it is NOT IMPORTABLE:
    #   ImportError: pyarrow requires NumPy 2.0 or newer, found 1.26.4
    # chai_lab pins `numpy~=1.21`, so its own two dependencies are mutually
    # unsatisfiable at import time. pandas then reports the engine as
    # "Missing optional dependency 'pyarrow'", which sends you looking for an
    # absent package rather than an unusable one, and `pip install pyarrow`
    # answers "Requirement already satisfied" and changes nothing.
    #
    # So the fix is a DOWNGRADE, not an install. pyarrow 17.0.0 is the last
    # release that declares numpy>=1.16.6; 18.0.0 onward declare no numpy pin
    # and enforce numpy>=2 at import.
    #
    # --force-reinstall because 26.0.1 already satisfies a bare requirement and
    # a plain install is a no-op. --no-deps WITH it, because pyarrow 17
    # declares `numpy>=1.16.6` and a force-reinstall re-resolves that to the
    # newest numpy: measured 2026-11-03, the first attempt here pulled numpy
    # 2.5.4 and tripped the numpy assert below. Both flags are load-bearing and
    # neither is sufficient alone.
    #
    # This gap is in the entry as the registry ships it, not something the
    # torch override above introduced: chai1_2.sif fails the same import. It
    # surfaced here only because this workstation produces real alignments
    # through the ColabFold endpoint and hands them to the fold, and a
    # single-sequence run never reaches the parquet path.
    pip install --no-cache-dir --force-reinstall --no-deps "pyarrow==17.0.0"

    # Assert PROPERTIES, not imports (tools/README.md).
    #
    # USE _cuda_getArchFlags(), NOT get_arch_list(). Measured 2026-11-03: with
    # no CUDA device visible -- which is every %post, since a build gets no
    # --nv -- torch.cuda.get_arch_list() returns an EMPTY LIST while
    # torch._C._cuda_getArchFlags() reads the build metadata and answers
    # correctly. An assert on the former passes vacuously or fails wrongly
    # depending on which way you write it, and it fails wrongly here.
    python - <<'PY'
import sys

import numpy
import torch

arch = torch._C._cuda_getArchFlags()
print("python", sys.version.split()[0], "torch", torch.__version__, "numpy", numpy.__version__)
print("arch flags:", arch)

major, minor = (int(x) for x in torch.__version__.split(".")[:2])
assert (major, minor) >= (2, 7), (
    f"torch {torch.__version__} is inside chai_lab's <2.7 pin, which is the "
    "version that has no sm_120. The override did not take."
)
assert "sm_120" in (arch or ""), (
    f"no sm_120 in {arch!r}. This image exists ONLY to carry sm_120 kernels; "
    "without them it is chai1_2 with a dependency conflict."
)
assert numpy.__version__.startswith("1."), f"chai_lab wants numpy 1.x, got {numpy.__version__}"

# Assert the parquet ROUND TRIP, not `import pyarrow`. The failure this guards
# is raised by pandas when it cannot find an engine, so the engine being
# importable is not the claim -- pandas being able to use it is.
import tempfile

import pandas as pd

with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as _d:
    _f = _d + "/t.pqt"
    pd.DataFrame({"sequence": ["ACDE"], "source_database": ["uniref90"]}).to_parquet(_f)
    assert list(pd.read_parquet(_f)["sequence"]) == ["ACDE"]
print("parquet round trip ok")
PY

    # chai_lab defaults CHAI_DOWNLOADS_DIR to <site-packages>/chai_lab/downloads,
    # which is inside the image and therefore READ-ONLY at run time.
    mkdir -p /tmp/chai-downloads

%environment
    export LC_ALL=C.UTF-8
    export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1
    export CHAI_DOWNLOADS_DIR="${CHAI_DOWNLOADS_DIR:-/tmp/chai-downloads}"

%test
    set -eu
    python -c "import chai_lab, torch; print('chai_lab ok, torch', torch.__version__)"
    command -v chai-lab

    # The whole point of this image, re-checked as an image rather than during %post.
    python - <<'PY'
import torch
arch = torch._C._cuda_getArchFlags()
assert "sm_120" in (arch or ""), f"no sm_120 in {arch!r}"
print("arch ok:", arch)
PY

    test -n "${CHAI_DOWNLOADS_DIR:-}" || { echo "CHAI_DOWNLOADS_DIR unset"; exit 1; }
    mkdir -p "$CHAI_DOWNLOADS_DIR"
    touch "$CHAI_DOWNLOADS_DIR/.writable" && rm -f "$CHAI_DOWNLOADS_DIR/.writable" \
        || { echo "CHAI_DOWNLOADS_DIR=$CHAI_DOWNLOADS_DIR is not writable"; exit 1; }
    echo "downloads dir $CHAI_DOWNLOADS_DIR is writable"

    # --- templates: the aligner, and that it actually ALIGNS -----------------
    # A kalign that is present and wrong produces a fold that exits zero and
    # silently used no templates, because chai_lab turns a non-zero kalign exit
    # into `return None` and the template loader reads that as "no hit".
    command -v kalign
    kalign --version 2>&1 | head -1
    python - <<'PY'
from chai_lab.tools.kalign import kalign_query_to_reference

ref = "MKTAYIAKQRQISFVKSHFSRQLEERLGLIEDSEATKG"
query = "MKTAYIAKQRQISFVKSHFRQLEERLGLIE"

aln = kalign_query_to_reference(ref=ref, query=query)
assert aln is not None, (
    "kalign_query_to_reference returned None: kalign ran and failed. This is the "
    "silent path -- every template hit would be dropped and the fold would still "
    "exit zero and write a structure."
)
assert len(aln.reference_aligned) == len(aln.query_aligned), (
    f"ragged alignment: {len(aln.reference_aligned)} vs {len(aln.query_aligned)}"
)
assert aln.reference_aligned.replace("-", "") == ref, "kalign mangled the reference"
assert aln.query_aligned.replace("-", "") == query, "kalign mangled the query"
assert "-" in aln.query_aligned, "expected the shorter query to be gapped against ref"
print("kalign alignment ok:", aln.query_aligned)
PY

%runscript
    exec chai-lab "$@"

%help
    Chai-1 0.6.1 on torch 2.7.0+cu128, so it runs on sm_120 (Blackwell).

      chpc-tool exec chai1 -- chai-lab fold input.fasta out/

    This image installs torch ONE MINOR VERSION PAST chai_lab's declared
    `<2.7` ceiling, deliberately, because 0.6.1 is the last release and the
    versions inside that ceiling ship neither sm_120 kernels nor PTX to JIT
    from. See the note at the top of the .def.

    A passing test is not permission to believe a fold from this image. Chai-1
    is the predictor whose wrong attachment moved ipTM by 0.012, so a covalent
    fold must land in the 1.636-1.770 A warhead band before its results are
    used.
